1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,286
Coach: An account of an action becomes more useful when the listener can understand the choice behind it.

2
00:00:07,286 --> 00:00:12,428
Coach: Explain what you knew, what was uncertain and why that uncertainty mattered,

3
00:00:12,428 --> 00:00:16,714
Coach: without turning a decision into proof of a successful outcome.

4
00:00:17,064 --> 00:00:21,350
Coach: In the fictional guide case, the participant drew the map,

5
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:24,778
Coach: Maya listed times and Noah assembled the guide.

6
00:00:24,778 --> 00:00:26,921
Coach: The day before the event,

7
00:00:26,921 --> 00:00:31,635
Coach: the map said Studio B while the schedule said Blue Room.

8
00:00:31,635 --> 00:00:36,778
Coach: The participant did not know whether those names meant the same room.

9
00:00:37,128 --> 00:00:40,557
Coach: The participant asked the coordinator instead of guessing.

10
00:00:40,557 --> 00:00:41,842
Coach: Confirmation came afterward:

11
00:00:41,842 --> 00:00:47,842
Coach: both names referred to the same room, and the door sign said Blue Room.

12
00:00:47,842 --> 00:00:52,985
Coach: The map label was then changed and the other volunteers were told.

13
00:00:53,335 --> 00:00:57,621
Coach: The stated reasoning is that conflicting labels could confuse visitors.

14
00:00:57,621 --> 00:01:02,335
Coach: Checking the door-sign name avoided choosing a label without verification.

15
00:01:02,335 --> 00:01:04,906
Coach: This explains why the participant asked;

16
00:01:04,906 --> 00:01:09,192
Coach: it does not measure how many visitors the change helped.

17
00:01:09,542 --> 00:01:12,542
Coach: Keep the information in the right order.

18
00:01:12,542 --> 00:01:16,828
Coach: Knowing the correct door-sign name after asking cannot be

19
00:01:16,828 --> 00:01:19,828
Coach: treated as something the participant knew beforehand.

20
00:01:19,828 --> 00:01:23,256
Coach: A clear account makes the original uncertainty visible.

21
00:01:23,606 --> 00:01:26,177
Coach: Privately identify what made checking relevant.

22
00:01:26,177 --> 00:01:30,034
Coach: Which inconsistency had been noticed, and what remained unknown?

23
00:01:30,034 --> 00:01:34,320
Coach: Use the supplied facts rather than inventing an earlier complaint

24
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:37,320
Coach: or a visitor who was already lost.

25
00:01:45,670 --> 00:01:51,670
Maya: Why did you ask the coordinator rather than choose one of the names yourself?

26
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:53,734
Coach: Compare three possible replies.

27
00:01:53,734 --> 00:01:58,449
Coach: Look for a connection between the information available at the time

28
00:01:58,449 --> 00:02:00,592
Coach: and the choice to check.

29
00:02:00,592 --> 00:02:06,163
Coach: Naming the action again is not yet a full explanation of that choice.

30
00:02:06,513 --> 00:02:11,227
Coach: Check whether each reply explains the choice or simply announces confidence.

31
00:02:11,227 --> 00:02:16,799
Coach: A later outcome cannot replace the information available when the decision was made.

32
00:02:16,799 --> 00:02:21,513
Coach: Keep that distinction in mind as you compare the three replies.

33
00:02:29,863 --> 00:02:35,863
You: The labels differed, and I did not know whether they meant the same room.

34
00:02:35,863 --> 00:02:41,434
You: I asked the coordinator so I would not choose a name by guessing.

35
00:02:41,784 --> 00:02:45,641
Maya: How did the coordinator’s confirmation affect what you changed?

36
00:02:45,991 --> 00:02:50,277
Coach: This reply explains the choice using the information available then.

37
00:02:50,277 --> 00:02:54,991
Coach: It leaves the confirmation and later observation in their proper places.

38
00:02:54,991 --> 00:02:57,562
Coach: A listener can examine the reasoning

39
00:02:57,562 --> 00:03:00,991
Coach: without being asked to accept an invented achievement.

40
00:03:01,341 --> 00:03:04,770
Coach: The shared facts support a simple decision explanation:

41
00:03:04,770 --> 00:03:07,770
Coach: the labels conflicted, their relationship was uncertain,

42
00:03:07,770 --> 00:03:10,341
Coach: and checking avoided an unverified choice.

43
00:03:10,341 --> 00:03:13,341
Coach: The later confirmation allowed a specific update.

44
00:03:13,341 --> 00:03:17,627
Coach: None of that establishes a measured improvement for all visitors.

45
00:03:17,977 --> 00:03:22,263
Coach: A reason can include a concern about what might happen.

46
00:03:22,263 --> 00:03:26,548
Coach: Say could confuse visitors when that is the stated concern.

47
00:03:26,548 --> 00:03:32,977
Coach: Do not change it into visitors were confused unless the account actually contains that observation.

48
00:03:33,327 --> 00:03:35,041
Coach: For a private attempt,

49
00:03:35,041 --> 00:03:40,613
Coach: explain the decision in this labelled case or a truthful choice you remember.

50
00:03:40,613 --> 00:03:45,327
Coach: Name the information that mattered and what you did not know.

51
00:03:45,327 --> 00:03:48,756
Coach: You do not need the same sentence pattern.

52
00:03:49,106 --> 00:03:53,392
Coach: If you cannot remember exactly why you made a choice,

53
00:03:53,392 --> 00:03:57,677
Coach: acknowledge that limit instead of adding a persuasive reason afterwards.

54
00:03:57,677 --> 00:04:01,535
Coach: You can choose another episode you remember more clearly,

55
00:04:01,535 --> 00:04:04,106
Coach: or continue examining the supplied example.

56
00:04:12,456 --> 00:04:17,599
Coach: If you formed a version, check its time order and its claims.

57
00:04:17,599 --> 00:04:22,742
Coach: Did you explain the original uncertainty, or add something known only later?

58
00:04:22,742 --> 00:04:28,742
Coach: Next, we will describe results with the same attention to what the evidence supports.
